w: Our God, our help in a- ges past, Our hope for years to come,
w: Un- der the sha- dow of Thy throne Thy saints have dwelt se- cure;
w: Be- fore the hills in or- der stood, Or earth re- ceived her frame,
w: Thy Word com- mands our flesh to dust, 'Re- turn, ye sons of men:'
w: A thou- sand a- ges in Thy sight Are like an eve- ning gone;
w: The bus- y tribes of flesh and blood, With all their lives and cares,
w: Time, like an ev- er roll- ing stream, Bears all its sons a- way;
w: Like flow'- ry fields the na- tions stand Pleased with the morn- ing light;
w: Our God, our help in a- ges past, Our hope for years to come,
B | c A d B | c A B G | A c d B | c3 |]
w: Our shel- ter from the storm- y blast, And our e- ter- nal home.
w: Suf- fic- ient is Thine arm a- lone, And our de- fense is sure.
w: From ev- er- last- ing Thou art God, To end- less years the same.
w: All na- tions rose from earth at first, And turn to earth a- gain.
w: Short as the watch that ends the night Be- fore the ris- ing sun.
w: Are car- ried down- wards by the flood, And lost in foll'- wing years.
w: They fly, for- got- ten, as a dream Dies at the op'- ning day.
w: The flow'rs be- neath the mow- er's hand Lie with'- ring ere 'tis night.
w: Be Thou our guard while troub- les last, And our e- ter- nal home.
